WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.260
- This week on 'Couch Tomatoes,'
we're talking Apple TV+'s

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:05.960
new original shows, like 'The
Morning Show,' 'Servant,'

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.650
ans 'For All Mankind,' and
giving you our Fresh Picks

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:10.350
on what else to watch.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.370
Hello friends, welcome
back to 'Couch Tomatoes,'

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:13.760
sponsored by Freschetta pizza.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.740
I'm your host, Naz Parez,
and today on the show

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.713
we're diving deep into Apple TV+.

00:00:17.713 --> 00:00:19.890
And here to help me break it all down

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.100
is TV host Heather Gardner.
- Hi.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.195
- [Naz] TV critic at
IndieWire, Ben Travers.

00:00:24.195 --> 00:00:25.028
- Hello.

00:00:25.028 --> 00:00:26.307
- [Naz] And TV host, Jackie Iadonisi.

00:00:26.307 --> 00:00:27.140
- Hello

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.162
- [Naz] What's up guys, you excited?

00:00:28.162 --> 00:00:29.310
- Yes.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.143
- I'm pumped.

00:00:30.143 --> 00:00:31.720
- I am stoked to talk about this,

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.070
because the new streaming service debuted

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.300
on November first at $4.99 a month.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.830
I feel like most of my
friends have iPhones,

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.550
but a lot of people don't
know about Apple TV+,

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:42.650
or don't have it.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.830
So if that's you, we're gonna
help you guys out today,

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.590
'cause we're gonna answer the question,

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.530
does Apple TV+ have
enough compelling content

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.510
to be worth it right now?

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:52.835
Hopefully by the end of the episode.

00:00:52.835 --> 00:00:55.280
But before we do that,
I wanna know what shows

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.080
stood out to you guys right off the bat.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.540
- Oh, I was most excited, and I subscribed

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.611
the very first day, and was so excited

00:01:01.611 --> 00:01:03.660
because of 'The Morning Show.'

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.320
That was the one that drew me in,

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.480
it was Jennifer Aniston's
return to television.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.110
Oh, I watched it that
night, that Friday night.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:12.009
- Yeah, no that was.

00:01:12.009 --> 00:01:13.209
(laughing)

00:01:13.209 --> 00:01:14.109
She said, and I was like.

00:01:14.109 --> 00:01:16.217
(chatter and yelling)

00:01:16.217 --> 00:01:18.670
Got the Apple TV+ right when it came out,

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.377
and watched 'The Morning
Show' right off the bat.

00:01:20.377 --> 00:01:22.130
- It's the reason, it's the
reason that hooked me in.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:22.963
- Yeah.

00:01:22.963 --> 00:01:23.796
- Who needs a 'Friends' reboot,

00:01:23.796 --> 00:01:24.820
when we got Jen on 'The Morning Show'?

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:25.653
- Okay.

00:01:25.653 --> 00:01:26.493
- Wait a minute.
(laughing)

00:01:26.493 --> 00:01:29.390
- What show stood out to you, Ben?

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:30.570
- Well, I mean, it's 'The Morning Show.'

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.670
Like 'The Morning Show'
was their premier program.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.520
That was the one that
everybody was supposed to be

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.100
looking forward to, that
everybody was excited about.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.800
You had Reese Witherspoon,
you had Jennifer Aniston,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.280
You had Mimi Leder, you had Kerry Ehrin.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.220
Like all of these people
had to make a good show.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:43.600
Like, they just had to make a good show.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.452
And then we watched it, and it was like.

00:01:45.452 --> 00:01:46.884
- Yeah, well, we'll get into that, yeah.

00:01:46.884 --> 00:01:48.440
(laughing)

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.030
- Jackie, what show stood out to you?

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:51.950
- I was really excited to see 'Dickinson.'

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.450
I love period pieces, and
it kind of intrigued me

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.010
that they were making
this modern take on it.

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:01.000
So I wasn't quite sure what to expect,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.381
but I was really excited about that one.

00:02:02.381 --> 00:02:03.214
- Yeah.

00:02:03.214 --> 00:02:04.680
- I love 'Dickinson,' it's so dope.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.950
And speaking of it, you and
I actually spoke earlier

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.090
about fun ways to set the tone at home

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.610
when you're watching a period piece.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:11.690
So check it out.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:14.880
- All right, Naz, so I know
how much you love shows

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:15.761
from different time periods, right?

00:02:15.761 --> 00:02:17.723
- Jackie, we talk about this all the time.

00:02:17.723 --> 00:02:19.660
I love the fashion, I love the trends.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.830
I just feel like everything
was so much fancier back then.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.250
- So much fancier, but you know

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.673
what I love the most, actually?

00:02:24.673 --> 00:02:25.770
Was the meals.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:26.603
- You know we can eat.

00:02:26.603 --> 00:02:27.463
- You know we can eat.

00:02:27.463 --> 00:02:30.380
- (laughing) Which is why
I made you this Frechetta

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.620
brick oven spinach and mushroom pizza.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.280
The mushrooms, the spinach,
the mozzarella cheese,

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.640
all of it together is
basically like a 12-course meal

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.026
within itself, and what sounds
fancier than brick oven?

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.640
- Nothing.
- Exactly.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.410
And I love to pair it with wine,

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.980
and this fancy tableware,
'cause you know I'm extra.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:46.813
- You are extra.

00:02:46.813 --> 00:02:48.270
But you know what?
I kinda don't mind it,

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.580
'cause now I feel like a proper lady.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:51.764
- You are a proper lady, Jackie.

00:02:51.764 --> 00:02:52.597
- [Jackie] Oh, thank you.

00:02:52.597 --> 00:02:53.660
- Cheers to that.
- We should.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.090
But wait, how does a
proper lady eat her pizza?

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.150
- With her pinky up.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:57.983
- [Jackie] Oh.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.330
- All right guys, let's
dive deep into Apple TV+'s

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.672
latest and greatest, the show everyone's

00:03:05.672 --> 00:03:07.780
talking about right now, 'Servant.'

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.490
Currently highest-rated out
of all the Apple TV+ shows,

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:15.120
and Certified Fresh with
an 83% on the Tomatometer.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.410
The show follows a couple
coping with the loss

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.290
of their son, Jericho,
but still pretending

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.810
that he's still alive, so creepy.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:21.643
- It is so creepy.

00:03:21.643 --> 00:03:22.476
- So creepy.

00:03:22.476 --> 00:03:24.880
Ben, what can we expect from this series?

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.554
- I think creepy is
probably the right word.

00:03:26.554 --> 00:03:28.670
(laughing blocks out speech)

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:30.900
- The doll's eyes, they just
never close, it's very--

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.318
- [Jackie] Scary.

00:03:32.318 --> 00:03:34.940
- It sets the tone so well,
because the premise itself

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.851
is so unusual. And like
Shyamalan talked about

00:03:37.851 --> 00:03:39.150
before the show came out,

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.955
it's based on this real form of therapy,

00:03:40.955 --> 00:03:42.760
in which if you lose a child,

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.740
you can use this kind of
baby as a replacement,

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:47.300
or fake baby, doll,
whatever we wanna call it,

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.900
as a replacement to kind
of get through your grief,

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:51.820
to try to progress forward.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.030
And yet, this couple
seems to be so stagnant

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.360
that they bring in an actual servant

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:58.800
to take care of the baby
when they don't need one,

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.290
because it's not real.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:01.787
So it's this great
premise where you're like,

00:04:01.787 --> 00:04:03.340
"Oh my gosh, what's gonna happen here?"

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.002
What happened to the baby?

00:04:05.002 --> 00:04:06.510
Where's the servant coming from?

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.700
Why is she going along with it?

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.190
It holds on for a pretty
good amount of time,

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.340
but eventually you just
kind of lose the thread.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:15.970
Like it didn't really
connect for me enough

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:18.310
to make it worth
finishing all 10 episodes.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:19.330
- How many did you see?

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:21.239
- I mean, I finished all 10
episodes because I had to.

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:22.381
(laughing)

00:04:22.381 --> 00:04:23.214
- Okay.

00:04:23.214 --> 00:04:24.610
- And I feel like you have to
go through the whole thing.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:25.880
- Where do you feel like
it dropped off for you?

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:27.890
- I feel like right in the
middle, you started to feel like,

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.490
okay, you could pick up
the pace a little bit.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:30.810
Or I wanna know more about this,

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:32.750
or this doesn't seem to be as
important as it needs to be.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:35.440
But, you're creeped out throughout.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.191
Like it's a pretty strange
show throughout its entire run,

00:04:39.191 --> 00:04:41.650
and there's great
performances within there.

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:43.440
It's beautiful to look
at, they do a great job

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.746
with that one scene, the one setting.

00:04:45.746 --> 00:04:48.470
The fact that it all takes
place in this big, old,

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:51.100
Philadelphia house, like a
little bit outside of it.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.440
That helps kind of create
this eerie vibe throughout,

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:54.760
but it just kinda, I don't know.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.360
It just kind of fizzled for me at the end.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.800
It wasn't scary enough if it
was gonna be a horror show.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:04:59.633
- Right.

00:04:59.633 --> 00:05:02.570
See for me, this show
made Apple TV+ worth it.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:04.170
Like before this show...

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.580
Because it released later
on, November 28th it released

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.440
three episodes, we have
three episodes right now.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.230
And I was just like, this is what we love

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.600
from M. Night Shyamalan, you know?

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.560
Like the shots of the
hallways, the staircase,

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.410
the big twist, it's like
creepy but also hilarious.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:19.810
Like when he drops the
doll I was dying laughing.

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:21.880
And I feel like all those
questions you were saying,

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.610
like I feel like it built
intrigue right off the bat.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:26.246
Shyamalan opened up and said that he needs

00:05:26.246 --> 00:05:29.092
about six seasons to tell
the full story of Servant,

00:05:29.092 --> 00:05:30.350
which I thought was interesting.

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:32.090
But it also got renewed
for a second season,

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:33.180
even before its premiere.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.430
So if you wanna jump in, I
think this one's worth it.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:37.370
But let's move on to the
show that I feel like

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.155
you guys are most excited to talk about.

00:05:39.155 --> 00:05:41.487
Everyone at home was so stoked on

00:05:41.487 --> 00:05:43.660
the most anticipated, 'The Morning Show.'

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.270
Like you said, starring Jen Aniston,

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:46.700
Steve Carell, Reese Witherspoon.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.360
So this shows follows the
aftermath of a morning show

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:52.910
after its main host gets
fired for sexual misconduct.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.630
63% on the Tomatometer, mixed reviews,

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.660
but a 94% Audience Score.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.330
So I feel like people,
everyone feels a type

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:00.930
of way about this show.
What did you guys think?

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:01.849
- I have some very strong feelings.

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:02.682
(laughing)

00:06:02.682 --> 00:06:03.515
- Let's talk this through.

00:06:03.515 --> 00:06:06.000
- Yeah, like this was
the reason that I had to

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.890
get Apple TV+, this was gonna hook me in.

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:11.540
I swore, no, not another
streaming service,

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:14.050
and yet I saw this pairing,
and I thought okay,

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:14.883
I can do it, I can do it.

00:06:14.883 --> 00:06:15.716
- [Jackie] Same.

00:06:15.716 --> 00:06:18.750
- I think for me, I just
over-hyped it so much in my brain.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:20.630
'Cause we live in this TV world.

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:22.530
'The Newsroom' is one of my all-time

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:23.800
favorite television shows.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.337
I love anything where I can see like

00:06:25.337 --> 00:06:28.670
myself reflected on TV, as
we all do, as we all like.

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:30.167
So I think I just over-hyped it so much,

00:06:30.167 --> 00:06:32.281
and I just felt like they just
only scratched the surface

00:06:32.281 --> 00:06:34.560
of all these really important issues.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.760
- You bring up a good point,
'cause my whole thing was like,

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:38.990
well, do I just love this
'cause I work in TV, you know?

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:40.520
What do you think, Ben and Jackie?

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:41.580
- I'm very much in the same boat.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.820
Like, the idea to me was,
I really wanted to get

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.210
invested in the people,
and I wanted to get

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:47.970
invested in the drama,
and kind of all of the

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:49.070
behind-the-scenes intrigue.

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:50.440
And I've had a lot of
people kind of talk to me

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:51.620
about 'The Morning Show'
in a sense of like,

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:52.660
well, it's so bad, it's good.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:55.370
Like, you can enjoy it almost
because it's just so soapy,

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:57.348
and you don't have to worry about it.

00:06:57.348 --> 00:06:58.560
I don't think it's effective at that.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.930
I don't even think they
get that level just right.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:03.170
The most compelling
aspect of the show to me

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:05.410
was just kind of trying to see
what they were gonna do next

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:07.430
in this not-complete train wreck,

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:08.933
but something that was
definitely off the rails.

00:07:08.933 --> 00:07:09.766
- Right.

00:07:09.766 --> 00:07:11.830
- And whenever you're
looking for those kind of

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:14.020
formal problems, I don't
think that's good TV.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:15.704
Like, I don't take a lot
of enjoyment in that.

00:07:15.704 --> 00:07:17.401
It straightens out a bit
toward the end, but yeah.

00:07:17.401 --> 00:07:19.493
It didn't really connect.

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:21.810
- I don't know, I'm kind of opposite.

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:23.391
I really enjoyed it, and I don't know if

00:07:23.391 --> 00:07:25.080
kind of like what you said, Heather,

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:26.670
because we're in the field.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:27.887
We kind of relate more to it.

00:07:27.887 --> 00:07:30.540
But I really did enjoy the storyline,

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:33.875
and I love the relationships
in this series as well.

00:07:33.875 --> 00:07:35.960
But I think it did drop off a little bit.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:38.620
I didn't really understand
the tone of the show.

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:41.160
One minute it's like this
serious conversation.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.500
The next it's like
overdramatic, and the dialogue,

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:45.120
it just doesn't fit very well.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.230
So it wasn't like one tone for me,

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:49.670
that's what kind of took
me back, but I loved it.

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:51.580
- They had so many issues to deal with.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:52.740
Of course the Me Too movement.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:54.930
They dealt with women's anger,
they dealt with diversity

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:58.270
in the workplace, and they
touch on all of those things,

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:00.530
but I didn't really get invested
in any one of those things,

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:02.731
because there was so much
that they wanted to say,

00:08:02.731 --> 00:08:04.720
that I just felt like
they didn't say anything.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:05.553
- I agree.

00:08:05.553 --> 00:08:07.414
And for me, the most interesting character

00:08:07.414 --> 00:08:10.660
was Mitch Kessler, and I'm
like, he's the most complex,

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:12.830
but he also is accused
of sexual misconduct.

00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:15.888
And then you have some
diverse ancillary characters

00:08:15.888 --> 00:08:18.480
that work in the newsroom,
but their stories

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:20.250
aren't that complicated and interesting.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:22.098
I'm like, why don't I
care about these people?

00:08:22.098 --> 00:08:24.730
I love the show, I
really love watching it,

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:25.563
I'm not gonna lie.

00:08:25.563 --> 00:08:28.190
I get excited every Friday,
but there's a little part of me

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:29.210
that's like, I think I just hoped

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:30.450
it was a little bit better.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:31.283
- Definitely.

00:08:31.283 --> 00:08:33.260
- I think Mitch Kessler is
such a problem for this show,

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:34.860
because Steve Carell is a great actor,

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:36.390
and you always wanna watch Steve Carell.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:37.960
And the idea of kind of casting somebody

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:39.457
who's kind of innately
lovable and that you

00:08:39.457 --> 00:08:41.077
really wanna watch a TV show about,

00:08:41.077 --> 00:08:43.270
I think that's great
for this kind of person,

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:46.010
because they're trying to
show why that's not okay.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:47.197
Like why the behind the scenes dramas,

00:08:47.197 --> 00:08:48.790
the real problem here.

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:51.480
But there's so much
pity and empathy evoked

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:52.920
for this guy who is a monster,

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:54.720
like who did something really terrible.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:55.553
- Yeah.

00:08:55.553 --> 00:08:57.420
- By the end of this first season,

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.510
they kind of come around on that to show

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:01.130
why they spent so much time with him,

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:03.390
but for those first five,
six, seven episodes,

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:06.600
you don't really understand
why you're feeling that way.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.430
And then even by the
end, you kinda wanna know

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:09.740
more about him, even though you shouldn't.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:10.910
Even though you should be writing him off,

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:12.605
and being like let's move
on, this isn't a good dude.

00:09:12.605 --> 00:09:13.610
- Interesting.

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:16.400
Well, I would like to move on to a show

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:19.207
I am super excited to talk
about - you guys like that?

00:09:19.207 --> 00:09:20.658
(laughing blocking out speech)
- That was great.

00:09:20.658 --> 00:09:21.491
(laughing)

00:09:21.491 --> 00:09:25.250
Let's talk about 'See,'
lowest-rated on the Tomatometer

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:28.300
at 41%, audiences are liking this show.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:31.240
Stars Jason Momoa, takes place
in an alternate universe,

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.280
600 years into the future.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.530
He is the father of these twins that have

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:37.810
the ability to see, unlike
everyone else in the show.

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:40.640
And he's sort of trying to
protect them and his tribe

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:42.790
from this queen that
wants to destroy them.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:48.050
The narrative was so convoluted
for me that I was like,

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:48.920
I don't know what's happening.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:51.677
But Ben, do you think this
show is worth watching?

00:09:51.677 --> 00:09:55.545
- I was one of the few people
who thought it was actually

00:09:55.545 --> 00:10:00.080
so silly and weird, that it
was kind of worth investing in

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:01.441
if that was something
you were looking for.

00:10:01.441 --> 00:10:03.080
Like if you were looking
for a fantasy show

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:04.592
that was trying to build
this strange new world.

00:10:04.592 --> 00:10:05.780
It was like, okay.

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:07.670
Like, you can give that a shot,
and see it this is for you.

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:08.925
I still don't think it's good.

00:10:08.925 --> 00:10:09.758
- Yeah.

00:10:09.758 --> 00:10:12.925
- I did not like this show whatsoever.

00:10:14.923 --> 00:10:15.898
- A lot of it is just confusing.

00:10:15.898 --> 00:10:17.547
- I was like, okay, I have to watch.

00:10:17.547 --> 00:10:18.381
- It's so confusing.

00:10:18.381 --> 00:10:19.214
- I have to watch a couple episodes.

00:10:19.214 --> 00:10:20.540
We're done now, okay great, thank you.

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:22.890
- When I saw the storyline
and what it was about,

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:25.840
I was just like this is
a recipe for disaster.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.840
But to what you're saying,
there was something about it

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:30.049
that was intriguing that I
couldn't stop watching it.

00:10:30.049 --> 00:10:32.410
I kept watching episode after episode,

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:33.573
I was invested in the characters.

00:10:33.573 --> 00:10:34.406
- [Naz] Really?

00:10:34.406 --> 00:10:35.480
- Even though on paper it's like,

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:37.454
okay, this world where everyone's blind.

00:10:37.454 --> 00:10:40.150
Like, it just didn't
really make sense to me.

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:41.780
But I really loved it.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:44.223
And I feel like they made it
for Jason Momoa specifically.

00:10:44.223 --> 00:10:45.650
- Of course, yeah.

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:48.540
- He was able to rock
climb, and I felt like

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.710
it was something that his family would do.

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:52.240
Every episode is something that he

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:54.290
would actually do in real life.

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:55.123
So I enjoyed that part about it.

00:10:55.123 --> 00:10:56.430
- Well, you mentioned the world.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:59.310
I feel like this does have
world building capabilities,

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.460
this show, even though it
wasn't for me personally.

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:03.650
Do you guys feel like it's
the next 'Game of Thrones'?

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:04.500
- Oh, no.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:05.700
- And it has those world, you know.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.580
- I mean, that's what
their attempt was for sure.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:09.760
Every single service,
every single network,

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:11.580
every single person in television

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:14.100
is trying to capture that
magic and that audience,

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:15.180
and there is a void left.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:18.370
And I watched the trailer, and
I wanted to fill that void,

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:20.987
because I loved that show so
much, and I couldn't do it.

00:11:20.987 --> 00:11:21.820
And you know what?

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:22.653
And I haven't found it

00:11:22.653 --> 00:11:23.520
on any other streaming service either.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:26.160
I don't really have the mode,
'cause it doesn't exist yet.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.952
- Yeah, I wanna support it, because I know

00:11:27.952 --> 00:11:30.470
some of the cast and crew are blind,

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:33.124
or have low vision, which
I thought was really dope.

00:11:33.124 --> 00:11:34.600
But I just wish it was...

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:36.020
I don't know, sometimes
you just wanna watch TV

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:37.633
and not have to think
and do math, you know?

00:11:37.633 --> 00:11:38.850
It's like, just give it to me.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:40.697
And I feel like I had to
figure things out way too much.

00:11:40.697 --> 00:11:42.320
- It was very complicated.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:44.690
I just left every single
episode feeling very confused

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:46.810
with more questions than answers,

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.420
and it just made me not
care so much about it.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:51.050
- It's really hard to
do the world-building

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:53.550
in a way where it all feels new and fresh,

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:55.010
but it also is kind of intuitive,

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:56.160
which I always thought was kind of weird,

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.200
because Apple prides itself on that.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:11:59.960
They're very intuitive
with their products,

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:01.893
with their iOS, with all of that.

00:12:01.893 --> 00:12:04.300
And yet the show itself
is just very confusing.

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:05.517
Like you can't just jump in and be like,

00:12:05.517 --> 00:12:06.767
"Oh, I get why they're doing that,

00:12:06.767 --> 00:12:08.040
I know why they made that choice."

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:09.733
It has to be explained, and
even then it doesn't make sense.

00:12:09.733 --> 00:12:11.640
- And yet we all have
iOS, and a lot of people

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:12.950
don't even know about 'See.'

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:15.137
But let's move from alternate universe

00:12:15.137 --> 00:12:18.277
to alternate history
with 'For All Mankind.'

00:12:18.277 --> 00:12:21.450
Now, this show is one of
the highest-praised shows

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:24.670
on Apple TV+, 71% on the Tomatometer.

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:26.484
Takes place in an alternate history

00:12:26.484 --> 00:12:29.220
where the United States
loses to the Soviets

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:31.150
in the space race, and
then falls second again

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:33.489
when the Soviets put a
woman on the moon first.

00:12:33.489 --> 00:12:37.140
Now, I was watching this
show, and I feel like a lot

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:39.020
of the brilliance is hidden in the pilot.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:40.540
Because it wasn't until the third episode

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:44.100
where I was like, "Oh, THIS
is a show, this is fantastic."

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:46.120
Is it fair to ask people to wait until

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.638
the third episode for
the story to pick up?

00:12:47.638 --> 00:12:48.580
What do you guys think?

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:49.550
- I thought the same thing.

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:51.560
This was one that I didn't
really know anything about,

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:52.670
and I'm not really drawn to like

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:54.420
history, period pieces anyway.

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:56.500
And so I was like, "Let's
just give it a shot."

00:12:56.500 --> 00:12:58.690
And it was the one that I liked the most

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:00.130
and was most surprised about.

00:13:00.130 --> 00:13:01.830
But I agree with you.

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:02.940
I was like, when are we gonna get there?

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:05.890
'Cause the trailer shows this
woman, female empowerment,

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:07.640
which I love to see on screen.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:10.190
I'm like, come on, when
are we gonna get there?

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:11.935
But I'm so excited every time it ends,

00:13:11.935 --> 00:13:14.095
because I just wanna press
play on the next one.

00:13:14.095 --> 00:13:16.116
- I feel like with so
many shows out there,

00:13:16.116 --> 00:13:17.919
you kinda do need to hit your audience

00:13:17.919 --> 00:13:20.270
right off the bat, or people will fall off

00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:22.410
to find something more
interesting to watch.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:24.380
So I feel like you gotta have something

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:26.170
in that first episode to draw you in.

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:27.390
And I feel like they just didn't

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:28.650
have that in the first episode.

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:30.540
I was kind of bored after to be honest.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:31.962
- So many of the choices
in the first episode

00:13:31.962 --> 00:13:34.420
seem to be antithetical to
what you're talking about.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:37.100
Like, the best character
in that first episode

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:39.850
is the woman who's sleeping
at NASA trying to...

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:40.683
- Yeah.

00:13:40.683 --> 00:13:41.516
(laughing)

00:13:41.516 --> 00:13:42.860
- So dedicated to everything she does,

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:44.430
and there's this one
mistake that she makes,

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:45.470
or she didn't speak up in time.

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:46.303
And you're like, okay,

00:13:46.303 --> 00:13:47.290
she's gonna correct that by the end of it.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:49.093
And instead they completely
abandon that storyline.

00:13:49.093 --> 00:13:50.890
They don't even follow up with her.

00:13:50.890 --> 00:13:53.670
And instead they're focused
on this Joel Kinnaman's

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:55.730
kind of failed pilot who makes a mistake,

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:57.800
and is kind of grumpy, and
angry, and yelling a lot.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:13:58.817
And you're like, "I've seen that guy,

00:13:58.817 --> 00:13:59.720
I don't care about him."

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:01.470
Like, let's go back to these other people.

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:03.060
- I just appreciated so much

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:04.920
the care that they took in the sets.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:06.045
- [Jackie] Absolutely.
- [Ben] That's true.

00:14:06.045 --> 00:14:06.878
- They--

00:14:06.878 --> 00:14:08.561
- [Naz] And the planes, and automobiles.

00:14:08.561 --> 00:14:11.340
But do you, Jackie, Ben,
do you guys like the show?

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:12.357
- No.

00:14:12.357 --> 00:14:14.137
(laughing)

00:14:14.137 --> 00:14:16.570
- I'm with Ben here, honestly.

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:18.827
- It is 'For All Mankind,'
except Ben Travers.

00:14:18.827 --> 00:14:20.500
(laughing)

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:23.990
For me, I think alternate-history
shows our tough,

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:24.980
especially with this one,

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.430
because there's so many lesser
known facts about this era,

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:28.478
that for me it was confusing.

00:14:28.478 --> 00:14:30.870
I like found myself Googling.

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:31.926
- No, same thing.

00:14:31.926 --> 00:14:33.426
But I actually found that--

00:14:33.426 --> 00:14:35.105
- Oh my god, I know I learned this.

00:14:35.105 --> 00:14:37.750
But like maybe we didn't?

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:39.130
It was interesting, though,
because I learned more

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:40.780
about Apollo 10, but anyways.

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:42.670
I'm glad to know how you
guys feel for the show.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:44.040
- The best feeling is off screen.

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:45.724
The best feeling was looking
up what really happened.

00:14:45.724 --> 00:14:46.557
(laughing)

00:14:46.557 --> 00:14:48.514
- I actually found that though, exciting.

00:14:48.514 --> 00:14:51.440
I found myself Googling,
did this really happen?

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:52.680
What really did happen?

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:54.510
But that to me just
kind of drew me in more.

00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:56.760
I learned, and I was excited about it.

00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:58.420
- Well Heather, we also
got a Florida education,

00:14:58.420 --> 00:14:59.253
so that's a tough.

00:14:59.253 --> 00:15:00.086
- Oh no. (laughing)

00:15:00.086 --> 00:15:00.919
- Wow. (laughing)

00:15:00.919 --> 00:15:01.823
- All right guys, let's...

00:15:01.823 --> 00:15:03.380
- Also, true. (laughing)

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:05.240
- Okay, so let's talk about Dickinson.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:07.620
I'm actually really excited
to talk about 'Dickinson,'

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:09.370
because it's the most bingeable show.

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:10.650
All 10 episodes came out

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:12.510
when the streaming service premiered.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:15.300
The episodes are 30 minutes
long, follows the life.

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:16.570
It's a highly stylized version

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:18.406
of the life of Emily Dickinson.

00:15:18.406 --> 00:15:19.902
Stars Hailee Steinfeld.

00:15:19.902 --> 00:15:20.913
And I feel like there's a lot of

00:15:20.913 --> 00:15:22.850
modern 'Sense And Sensibility.'

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:24.354
What did you guys think about 'Dickinson'?

00:15:24.354 --> 00:15:26.540
- Loved it, I blew through it.

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:31.540
I watched the entire 10
episode season in two days.

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:32.790
I loved it.

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:37.790
- I loved it, but I also
had mixed feelings about it.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:42.150
Because I love period
pieces, and I felt like.

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:43.960
I thought it was innovative that they were

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:46.680
mashing the two together, modern and past.

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:49.960
But why choose that
story and do it in such a

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:51.640
all over the place kind of way.

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:52.473
Do you know what I mean?

00:15:52.473 --> 00:15:54.090
- Well, I'm gonna push back and say

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:55.673
that I think it was
because Emily Dickinson,

00:15:55.673 --> 00:15:59.100
the real-life person,
was just really strange.

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:02.040
She was obsessed with death,
she was a reclusive person

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:03.818
in her home that really never left

00:16:03.818 --> 00:16:05.790
her father's house her entire life.

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:07.870
And she didn't get popular
until after her death.

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:09.483
Like, she was a pretty strange person,

00:16:09.483 --> 00:16:11.230
especially if you read her work.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:12.698
So to me, it actually felt like

00:16:12.698 --> 00:16:15.117
a very honoring way to describe her life.

00:16:15.117 --> 00:16:17.380
- And I feel like that's all
we learn about her school,

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:19.830
is she was this poet from
the 1800s, she never left

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:22.280
her room, her poems were
published afterwards.

00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:24.010
But what I love about this
show is that they pull

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:27.990
aspects of life, lesser
known aspects of her life,

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:28.823
and put it in the plot.

00:16:28.823 --> 00:16:30.380
Like the fact that
she's queer in the show,

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:31.280
I love that they explore that.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:32.113
Ben, what'd you think of--

00:16:32.113 --> 00:16:32.946
- No, I agree.

00:16:32.946 --> 00:16:34.420
I think it's kind of in
between those reactions.

00:16:34.420 --> 00:16:37.220
I do feel like what
they choose to focus on

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:40.493
in terms of Emily's backstory,
and how progressive she was,

00:16:40.493 --> 00:16:41.820
and the stuff that kind of gets

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:43.070
shoved at the side was great.

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:44.874
And I love the performances,
I love the design of it.

00:16:44.874 --> 00:16:47.452
And at the same time, a lot of
those kind of modern touches

00:16:47.452 --> 00:16:49.890
that they try to do,
especially with the language,

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:51.590
I don't know how well
they incorporate that.

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:53.600
Like, that kind of pulls
me out from time to time,

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:56.330
which made it not quite as
addictive as I wanted it to be.

00:16:56.330 --> 00:16:58.852
But by the time we got to the
third episode, you're flying.

00:16:58.852 --> 00:16:59.685
- I think--

00:16:59.685 --> 00:17:00.518
- That party scene.

00:17:00.518 --> 00:17:02.108
(yelling)

00:17:02.108 --> 00:17:04.430
- It was really good.

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:06.340
- If you just lean into the weirdness,

00:17:06.340 --> 00:17:07.180
because the show does.

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:09.550
And it does take a few
minutes and a few episodes

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:11.430
to kind of get into the weirdness.

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:12.540
But they go right in.

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:15.650
If you can just let go, and
lean in, and close your eyes,

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:17.240
and pretend that you're
just watching a meme

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:19.177
on the internet or something,
you're gonna love it,

00:17:19.177 --> 00:17:20.030
and that's what I did.

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:21.050
I just went with it.

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:21.883
- But that's what I feel like.

00:17:21.883 --> 00:17:24.162
I totally love weirdness,
and I'm all about that.

00:17:24.162 --> 00:17:26.010
And I wanted to lean into the weirdness.

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:29.110
But then it's also trying to go with the.

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:32.710
It just felt like there was, I
didn't know where to lean to.

